"""
CLN2 Epilepsy — Neuronal Ceroid Lipofuscinosis Type 2 / Late-Infantile Batten Disease
========================================================================================
40-patient cohort · CLN2 / TPP1 (11p15.4) · Autosomal recessive (AR)
TPP1 = Tripeptidyl Peptidase 1 — lysosomal serine protease; cleaves tripeptides from N-terminus of substrates
LOF → lysosomal TPP1 deficiency → accumulation of subunit c of mitochondrial ATP synthase (SCMAS) → autofluorescent
storage material (fingerprint profiles + curvilinear bodies + rectilinear complexes on EM) →
neuronal death (cortical + cerebellar + retinal) → CLN2 disease (Late-Infantile NCL)

ONLY NCL WITH APPROVED ENZYME REPLACEMENT THERAPY:
Cerliponase alfa (Brineura, BioMarin Pharmaceutical) — intracerebroventricular (ICV) enzyme replacement therapy
FDA-approved: April 2017 (first-ever approved treatment for any Batten disease)
EMA-approved: May 2017
Mechanism: recombinant human TPP1 administered directly into lateral ventricle every 2 weeks via
Ommaya reservoir → restores lysosomal TPP1 activity → slows SCMAS accumulation → delays neurological progression
PIVOTAL TRIAL: Schulz A et al. 2018 (NEJM 378:1861-1870) — showed ~2-fold slower disease progression vs. natural history

DIAGNOSTIC MANDATORY SEQUENCE:
Curvilinear bodies on EM (as seen in KCTD7 EPM3) → CLN2/TPP1 enzyme assay FIRST (days turnaround) →
CLN2 gene panel → KCTD7 sequencing only after CLN2 excluded
Critical: CLN2 is TREATABLE — missed/delayed diagnosis = lost treatment window

TPP1 PROTEIN BIOLOGY (LYSOSOMAL SERINE PROTEASE):
CLN2/TPP1 (11p15.4), Tripeptidyl Peptidase 1:
  - 563 aa preproprotein; ~66 kDa mature form; lysosomal serine protease (subtilisin-like)
  - Signal peptide aa 1-19 (ER targeting); propeptide aa 20-195 (activation/folding);
      mature enzyme aa 196-563 (catalytic triad: Ser475-Asp360-His272)
  - Activated by lysosomal acid pH — procTPP1 → active TPP1 via autocatalytic processing
  - Substrate: tripeptide cleavage from N-terminus of unfolded/denatured substrates in lysosome
  - Primary stored substrate: subunit c of mitochondrial ATP synthase (SCMAS, ceroid) →
      hydrophobic, auto-fluorescent, forms fingerprint/curvilinear/rectilinear EM patterns
  - Also degrades: angiotensin II, neuropeptides, SCMAS-related peptides
  - Expression: ubiquitous (all lysosomes); highest in neurons, retinal pigment epithelium
  - CLN2 LOF → TPP1 absence → SCMAS accumulates → autofluorescent ceroid storage →
      selective neuronal apoptosis (cortex, cerebellum, retina) → CLN2 disease
  - pLI ~0.94 (high intolerance to heterozygous LOF — strong AR selection)
  - OMIM: *607998 (TPP1 gene); #204500 (CLN2 disease)
  - Sleat DE et al. 1997 (Science 277:1802-1805) — CLN2 gene discovery (positional cloning)

CLN2 DISEASE (NEURONAL CEROID LIPOFUSCINOSIS TYPE 2 / LATE-INFANTILE BATTEN DISEASE):
  Disease: CLN2 disease — Late-Infantile Neuronal Ceroid Lipofuscinosis (LINCL)
    Also known as: Batten disease (specifically CLN2 subtype), Janský-Bielschowsky disease
    Gene: CLN2 / TPP1 (11p15.4)
    First report: Janský 1908 (clinical) / Bielschowsky 1913 (pathology);
      molecular basis: Sleat DE et al. 1997 (Science)
    Enzyme: Tripeptidyl Peptidase 1 (TPP1); assay: DBS or leukocyte TPP1 activity
    Storage: SCMAS + fingerprint profiles (dominant) + curvilinear bodies + rectilinear complexes
  Onset: 2-4 years (after normal early development); CLASSIC late-infantile phenotype
    Atypical forms: juvenile CLN2 (5-8 years onset with c.509-1G>C or milder variants)
    Initial presentation: seizures are usually FIRST symptom (2-4 years)
      - Generalised tonic-clonic seizures (GTCS) most common first seizure type
      - Followed within months by myoclonic seizures → atonic → absence → focal
      - Language regression co-occurs with or shortly after seizure onset
      - Motor deterioration: ataxia → spasticity → loss of ambulation → vegetative state
  DISTINGUISHING EEG FEATURE — GIANT SSPS (PATHOGNOMONIC):
    Giant Occipital Response to Single Slow Photic Stimulation (SSPS) at 1-3 Hz:
    - Standard EEG photic stimulation at 1-3 Hz → giant occipital response (amplitude >3× normal)
    - This SSPS response is PATHOGNOMONIC for CLN2 disease
    - Distinguishes CLN2 from other PMEs and from KCTD7 (KCTD7 has giant SEP/cortical response but NOT SSPS)
    - Must perform SSPS at 1-2 Hz in every child with late-infantile seizure onset
    - Disappears in late-stage disease when retinal function lost
  Neurological features:
    (1) Seizures — multiple types; tonic-clonic FIRST; then myoclonus, atonic, absence; NCSE common
    (2) Cognitive regression — language regression early; progressive dementia
    (3) Motor deterioration — cerebellar ataxia → pyramidal signs → spasticity → wheelchair
    (4) Visual failure — retinal degeneration; progressive visual loss → blindness; ERG flat
    (5) Behavioural features — aggression, sleep disturbance, anxiety early; progressive blunting
  Retinal involvement: retinal degeneration → pigmentary changes → ERG amplitude reduction →
    electroretinogram (ERG) flat by 4-6 years; ophthalmology essential
  MRI pattern: early = normal; then cerebellar atrophy (vermis first) + cerebral atrophy;
    T2/FLAIR white matter signal changes; thalamic involvement late; MRI serial monitoring
  Storage material (EM):
    Fingerprint profiles (FP) — DOMINANT pattern in CLN2 (curved laminar profiles resembling fingerprint)
    Curvilinear bodies (CB) — also present (can overlap with KCTD7 appearance)
    Rectilinear complexes — late stage
    Granular osmiophilic deposits (GROD) — less common in CLN2 (more CLN1)
    Auto-fluorescence: strongly auto-fluorescent under fluorescence microscopy
  Course: FATAL — rapid progression without treatment (wheelchair by 6-7y; vegetative 8-9y; death 8-12y)
    With cerliponase alfa treatment: significant slowing; some patients maintain ambulation 4+ extra years
  CLN2 vs KCTD7 key distinctions (critical for diagnostic pathway):
    CLN2: TPP1 enzyme assay ABNORMAL → CLN2 gene confirmed; KCTD7: TPP1 enzyme NORMAL → KCTD7 WES
    CLN2: giant SSPS response at 1-3 Hz; KCTD7: giant SEP / cortical myoclonus but no SSPS
    CLN2: TREATABLE (cerliponase alfa); KCTD7: symptomatic only
    CLN2: fingerprint profiles dominant; KCTD7: curvilinear bodies dominant
    CLN2: retinal degeneration + visual loss; KCTD7: vision preserved
    CLN2: wider seizure type variety; KCTD7: extreme stimulus-sensitive myoclonus dominant
  Epidemiology: ~1:100,000 births in Western countries; incidence higher in some Scandinavian populations;
    2 common variants account for ~60% alleles in European populations:
    c.523-1G>C (IVS5-1G>C) splice-site — most common worldwide
    c.509-1G>C (IVS4-1G>C) splice-site — associated with attenuated/juvenile phenotype
    Arginine-Arginine: p.Arg208* + p.Arg447His compound het — common European pair
"""


